From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 20:59:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] iommu: apple-dart: optionally save a DART without a
 sleep list

A consumer that gates itself before a firmware-driven sleep saves its DART
with apple_dart_sleep_prepare(), and the DART's own system sleep callbacks
then leave it alone until apple_dart_sleep_restore(). That only happens for
a DART with an apple,sleep-restore list. Without one the DART keeps its own
callbacks, and its noirq resume reads the translation registers.

On T8132 the SEP's DART has no such list, so when the SEP has gated itself
and the suspend is aborted before the SoC powers down, that noirq resume
reads a block which nothing powers back. The CPU can stall there for good,
past the point where the bring-up watchdog of apple-s2r is put back.

Add the sleep_save_unlisted parameter. With it set, an unlocked DART
without a sleep list is saved in apple_dart_sleep_prepare() too, and its
callbacks skip it as for one with a list. apple_dart_sleep_restore() then
clears the mark and, after a real sleep, restores it with the full resume
its noirq callback would otherwise have run. After a suspend that never
powered the SoC down nothing is written: nothing was lost.

The parameter is off by default; then nothing changes.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
---
 drivers/iommu/apple-dart.c | 41 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 37 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/iommu/apple-dart.c b/drivers/iommu/apple-dart.c
index c0ebe53693..b27ddb6ea2 100644
--- a/drivers/iommu/apple-dart.c
+++ b/drivers/iommu/apple-dart.c
@@ -369,6 +369,8 @@ struct apple_dart {
 	u32 power_retained : 1;
 	/* State a consumer saved for a firmware-driven suspend and will restore. */
 	u32 sleep_saved : 1;
+	/* Saved without a sleep list (sleep_save_unlisted): a full resume restores it. */
+	u32 sleep_unlisted : 1;
 	/* Locked roots may hold firmware slots that mirror Linux mappings. */
 	u32 fw_mirror : 1;
 	u32 fw_handoff : 1;
@@ -5757,14 +5759,30 @@ static void apple_dart_sleep_wait_tlb(struct apple_dart *dart)
 		dev_err(dart->dev, "TLB command still busy around a sleep: %#x\n", status);
 }
 
+static int apple_dart_resume(struct device *dev);
+
+/*
+ * Bring-up: a consumer that gates itself (the T8132 SEP) can have a DART
+ * without a sleep list, which apple_dart_sleep_prepare() then leaves to the
+ * DART's own callbacks. Their noirq resume reads its registers, also after a
+ * suspend aborted once the consumer had gated, when nothing powers that
+ * block back. With this set, such a DART is saved there too and left alone
+ * until apple_dart_sleep_restore(), which runs a full resume after a real
+ * sleep. Locked DARTs are not affected. Default off: nothing changes.
+ */
+static bool sleep_save_unlisted;
+module_param(sleep_save_unlisted, bool, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(sleep_save_unlisted, "Bring-up: let apple_dart_sleep_prepare() save an unlocked DART without an apple,sleep-restore list too, so that its system sleep callbacks leave it to its consumer (default: off)");
+
 /**
  * apple_dart_sleep_prepare() - Save a DART whose consumer is about to gate itself
  * @dev: the DART
  *
- * For a DART with apple,sleep-restore in its node. A consumer calls this before
+ * For a DART with apple,sleep-restore in its node, or, with sleep_save_unlisted
+ * set, for any unlocked one. A consumer calls this before
  * telling its firmware to sleep, while the DART's state can be read, and
  * apple_dart_sleep_restore() before starting that firmware again. The DART's own
- * callbacks then leave it alone. Without the property nothing happens here, and
+ * callbacks then leave it alone. Otherwise nothing happens here, and
  * they do what they always do.
  *
  * Return: 0, or -ENODEV if the DART driver isn't bound.
@@ -5774,13 +5792,16 @@ int apple_dart_sleep_prepare(struct device *dev)
 	struct apple_dart *dart = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
 	unsigned long flags;
 	unsigned int i;
+	bool unlisted;
 
 	if (!dart)
 		return -ENODEV;
-	if (!dart->sleep_restore.count)
+	unlisted = !dart->sleep_restore.count;
+	if (unlisted && (!READ_ONCE(sleep_save_unlisted) || dart->locked))
 		return 0;
 
-	apple_dart_sleep_cfg_read(dart, dart->sleep_cfg);
+	if (!unlisted)
+		apple_dart_sleep_cfg_read(dart, dart->sleep_cfg);
 	spin_lock_irqsave(&dart->lock, flags);
 	apple_dart_sleep_write(dart, &dart->sleep_prepare);
 	apple_dart_sleep_wait_tlb(dart);
@@ -5788,6 +5809,7 @@ int apple_dart_sleep_prepare(struct device *dev)
 	for (i = 0; i < BITS_TO_U32(dart->num_streams); i++)
 		dart->sleep_enable[i] = apple_dart_readl(dart, dart->hw->enable_streams + 4 * i);
 	dart->sleep_saved = true;
+	dart->sleep_unlisted = unlisted;
 	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&dart->lock, flags);
 
 	return 0;
@@ -5810,6 +5832,17 @@ void apple_dart_sleep_restore(struct device *dev)
 	if (!dart || !dart->sleep_saved)
 		return;
 
+	if (dart->sleep_unlisted) {
+		scoped_guard(spinlock_irqsave, &dart->lock) {
+			dart->sleep_saved = false;
+			dart->sleep_unlisted = false;
+		}
+		/* Nothing was lost with the power kept; after a real sleep, all of it. */
+		if (pm_resume_via_firmware() && apple_dart_resume(dev))
+			dev_err(dev, "restore before its consumer's restart failed\n");
+		return;
+	}
+
 	spin_lock_irqsave(&dart->lock, flags);
 	dart->sleep_saved = false;
 	if (pm_resume_via_firmware()) {
